{
  "id": "COMPOUND:982 - DISEASE:1057",
  "verbalization": "Hydrochlorothiazide, a thiazide diuretic, appears to influence Erythrokeratoderma en cocardes through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that hydrochlorothiazide affects ankyrin repeat domain 28, a gene product of Peroxiredoxin-like 2A, which is activated in M-CSF-stimulated monocytes. This pathway leads to the condition, indicating a potential role in cellular dysfunction or inflammation. Additionally, hydrochlorothiazide is linked to adhesion G protein-coupled receptor B3, which is associated with Linear sebaceous naevus sequence, further contributing to the development of Erythrokeratoderma en cocardes. The third pathway involves hydrochlorothiazide affecting deleted in azoospermia 3, a gene product of Zinc finger SWIM domain-containing protein 1, which also contributes to the condition. These findings suggest that hydrochlorothiazide may exert its effects through diverse molecular mechanisms, potentially involving immune or cellular signaling pathways. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship, with each contributing to the pathogenesis of Erythrokeratoderma en cocardes. The evidence supports a mechanistic link, though the exact role of each pathway remains to be fully elucidated.",
